- [More guides](/blog)', 'Finance', ARRAY['Finance', '401k calculator', '401k retirement calculator', '401k growth calculator'], 'published', 'CalcHub', '2026-11-20T04:34:43.059Z', '2026-11-21T12:38:31.138Z') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, excerpt = EXCLUDED.excerpt, content = EXCLUDED.content, category = EXCLUDED.category, tags = EXCLUDED.tags, status = EXCLUDED.status, author = EXCLUDED.author, updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at;
INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category, tags, status, author, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('add-business-days-to-date-guide', 'Add Business Days to Date: how it works, formula and examples', 'Add Business Days to Date: Add business days to a date, skipping weekends and holidays. Learn how to use it, the formula behind it, a worked example and…', 'The [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) is a free online tool on CalcHub. Add business days to a date, skipping weekends and holidays. Enter your start date, business days to add and holidays to skip (one date per line, YYYY-MM-DD) and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. Because it runs entirely in your browser, the values you type never leave your device, and you can use it as often as you like.

Add business days to a date, skipping Saturdays and Sundays — and any holidays you list. Delivery estimates, contract deadlines, invoice terms and project plans are usually counted in business days, not calendar days: “5 business days from Friday” lands the following Friday, not Wednesday. Enter the start date and how many business days to add; holidays go in one per line as YYYY-MM-DD dates. This guide explains how to use it, the formula it applies, and a worked example you can follow with your own numbers.

## How to use the Add Business Days to Date

Start by picking your start date, then entering your business days to add and finally typing or pasting your holidays to skip (one date per line, YYYY-MM-DD). The result updates as you type, so adjust any value and watch the answer change. If the answer is not what you expected, check the value you entered first — a missing digit or a wrong unit is the usual cause.

1. Enter the start date and how many business days to add.
2. Optionally list holidays to skip, one date per line (YYYY-MM-DD).
3. Read the result date — and how many calendar and weekend days it spans.

## Formula

Step forward one calendar day at a time, counting only Monday–Friday days that are not listed as holidays. For example, 5 business days from Friday 20 November 2026 is Friday 27 November 2026 — 7 calendar days, with 2 weekend days skipped.

That is the standard formula for this calculation, and it is the same one the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) applies to the values you enter. You can work it out by hand, but the calculator is faster when you want to try several values, and it removes the risk of a slip in the arithmetic.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. Adding 5 business days to Friday 20 November 2026 gives Friday 27 November 2026: the 21st and 22nd are the weekend, so the counted days are the 23rd, 24th, 25th, 26th and 27th. Try the same figures in the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves.

## What the result shows

With the default values (Start date: 2026-11-20, Business days to add: 5, Holidays to skip (one date per line, YYYY-MM-DD):), the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) returns Result date: Friday, November 27, 2026, Business days added: 5, Calendar days: 7, Weekend days skipped: 2. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) is worth a look before you finish.

## What counts as a business day

Monday to Friday, excluding public holidays. The start date itself is not counted — “1 business day from Friday” is Monday, not Friday. Different countries and industries have different weekends and holidays (some places work Sundays, some observe Friday as the weekend), so list any extra non-working days in the holidays box to match your own calendar.

## Deadlines and delivery estimates

“Allow 5 business days” is the standard language for deliveries, refunds and contract deadlines precisely because weekends do not count. Count carefully around long weekends: 5 business days before a bank holiday Monday is very different from 5 business days after one. When a deadline matters, add the holidays explicitly rather than assuming them.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- The start date is not counted — day 1 is the next business day.
- List public holidays one per line, as YYYY-MM-DD.
- Check the calendar days too, for planning around weekends.

## Frequently asked questions

### How do I add 5 business days to a date?

Count forward 5 Monday–Friday days, skipping weekends and holidays. 5 business days from Friday 20 November 2026 is Friday 27 November 2026. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### Does the start date count?

No. The start date is day zero — the first business day counted is the next working day after it. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### Can I skip public holidays too?

Yes — list them in the holidays box, one date per line in YYYY-MM-DD format, and they are skipped like weekends. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What is the difference between business days and calendar days?

Calendar days include weekends; business days are Monday–Friday only. 5 business days usually spans 7 calendar days. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

## Related tools

The [Age Calculator](/age-calculator), [Days Between Dates Calculator](/days-between-dates-calculator), [Date Calculator](/date-calculator) and [Time Calculator](/time-calculator) cover related calculations in the same category. Read the guides to the [Age Calculator](/blog/age-calculator-guide) and [Days Between Dates Calculator](/blog/days-between-dates-calculator-guide) for more detail on how those work.

## Try it now

- [Open the Add Business Days to Date](/add-business-days-to-date)
- [Age Calculator](/age-calculator)
- [Days Between Dates Calculator](/days-between-dates-calculator)
- [Date Calculator](/date-calculator)
- [Time Calculator](/time-calculator)
- [Browse all Date & Time tools](/tools?category=Date%20%26%20Time)
- [More guides](/blog)', 'Date & Time', ARRAY['Date & Time', 'add business days to date', 'business days calculator', 'add working days calculator'], 'published', 'CalcHub', '2026-11-20T02:47:35.094Z', '2026-11-21T12:38:31.138Z') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, excerpt = EXCLUDED.excerpt, content = EXCLUDED.content, category = EXCLUDED.category, tags = EXCLUDED.tags, status = EXCLUDED.status, author = EXCLUDED.author, updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at;
INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category, tags, status, author, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('add-prefix-suffix-guide', 'Add Prefix and Suffix: how it works, formula and examples', 'Add Prefix and Suffix: Add text to the start or end of every line — for lists, code and data. Learn how to use it, the formula behind it, a worked example and…', 'The [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) is a free online tool on CalcHub. Add text to the start or end of every line — for lists, code and data. Enter your lines (one item per line), prefix and suffix and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. Because it runs entirely in your browser, the values you type never leave your device, and you can use it as often as you like.

Add the same text to the start (prefix) or end (suffix) of every line at once. Paste a list — one item per line — and the tool wraps each line for you: bullet points, quotes around values, commas for code, HTML tags, file paths, or anything else you need repeated. Empty lines are skipped, and the original line order is preserved. This guide explains how to use it, the formula it applies, and a worked example you can follow with your own numbers.

## How to use the Add Prefix and Suffix

Start by typing or pasting your lines (one item per line), then typing or pasting your prefix and finally typing or pasting your suffix. The result updates as you type, so adjust any value and watch the answer change. If the answer is not what you expected, check the value you entered first — a missing digit or a wrong unit is the usual cause.

1. Paste your list, one item per line.
2. Enter the prefix (added to the start of each line) and the suffix (added to the end).
3. Copy the result — empty lines are skipped automatically.

## How it works

The [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) works straight from the values you enter: change an input and the result updates immediately, with no page reload and no waiting. Everything happens in your browser, so your entries never leave your device. Try it with a few different values and you will quickly get a feel for how the inputs move the result.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. With the prefix “• ” the lines apple, banana, cherry become “• apple”, “• banana”, “• cherry”. With the prefix ''"'' and the suffix ''",'' the same lines become ''"apple",'', ''"banana",'', ''"cherry",'' — ready to paste into code. Try the same figures in the [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves.

## What the result shows

With the default values (Lines (one item per line): apple banana cherry, Prefix: • , Suffix:), the [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) returns With prefix / suffix: • apple • banana • cherry, Lines processed: 3. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Word Counter](/word-counter) is worth a look before you finish.

## What it is used for

The most common uses are wrapping values in quotes for code ("apple", "banana"), adding commas to lists, prefixing file paths with a folder name, adding bullet characters or markdown list markers, and wrapping lines in HTML tags. Anything you would otherwise do by hand, line by line, is a candidate — the tool does in one click what would take minutes of repetitive editing.

## Working with code lists

To turn a plain list into a JavaScript array, set the prefix to a quote mark and the suffix to a quote mark plus a comma. To make a markdown list, use “- ” as the prefix. To build a SQL IN clause, use a quote as the prefix and “'',” as the suffix, then wrap the result in parentheses. The same idea works for CSV rows, CSS selectors and test data.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- One item per line — if your list is comma-separated, use the Sum Calculator''s list format or replace commas with new lines first.
- Leave the prefix or suffix empty if you only need one of them.
- Use the Text Formatter afterwards to fix the case of the whole list.

## Frequently asked questions

### What is a prefix and a suffix?

A prefix is text added to the beginning of each line; a suffix is text added to the end. For example, the prefix ''• '' turns ''apple'' into ''• apple''. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### Are empty lines kept?

No — blank lines are skipped so the output has no stray prefixes or suffixes on empty lines. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### Can I add HTML tags to each line?

Yes — set the prefix to <li> and the suffix to </li> and each line becomes a list item. Wrap the result in <ul>…</ul> yourself. Try it with your own numbers in the [Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What do I need to use the Add Prefix and Suffix?

Just Lines (one item per line), Prefix and Suffix. Enter those values and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. If you are unsure about a value, try a rough figure first and then refine it — the result updates instantly.

## Related tools

The [Word Counter](/word-counter), [Character Counter](/character-counter), [Reading Time Calculator](/reading-time-calculator) and [Remove Spaces](/remove-spaces) cover related calculations in the same category. Read the guides to the [Word Counter](/blog/word-counter-guide) and [Character Counter](/blog/character-counter-guide) for more detail on how those work.

## Try it now

- [Open the Add Prefix and Suffix](/add-prefix-suffix)
- [Word Counter](/word-counter)
- [Character Counter](/character-counter)
- [Reading Time Calculator](/reading-time-calculator)
- [Remove Spaces](/remove-spaces)
- [Browse all Text Tools tools](/tools?category=Text%20Tools)
- [More guides](/blog)', 'Text Tools', ARRAY['Text Tools', 'add prefix suffix', 'add prefix to each line', 'add suffix to lines'], 'published', 'CalcHub', '2026-11-20T03:41:01.114Z', '2026-11-21T12:38:31.138Z') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, excerpt = EXCLUDED.excerpt, content = EXCLUDED.content, category = EXCLUDED.category, tags = EXCLUDED.tags, status = EXCLUDED.status, author = EXCLUDED.author, updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at;
INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category, tags, status, author, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('age-calculator-guide', 'Age Calculator: how it works, formula and examples', 'Age Calculator: Exact age in years, months and days. Learn how to use it, the formula behind it, a worked example and answers to common questions.', 'The [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) is a free online tool on CalcHub. Exact age in years, months and days. Enter your date of birth and age on and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. Because it runs entirely in your browser, the values you type never leave your device, and you can use it as often as you like.

Calculate your exact age from your date of birth, down to the day, along with total days lived and a countdown to your next birthday. This guide explains how to use it, the formula it applies, and a worked example you can follow with your own numbers. Related Date & Time tools include [Days Between Dates Calculator](/days-between-dates-calculator), [Date Calculator](/date-calculator) and [Time Calculator](/time-calculator).

## How to use the Age Calculator

Start by picking your date of birth, then picking your age on. The result updates as you type, so adjust any value and watch the answer change. If the answer is not what you expected, check the value you entered first — a missing digit or a wrong unit is the usual cause. When you want a clean slate, Reset restores the defaults.

1. Pick your date of birth.
2. Pick your age on.
3. Check the result — it updates instantly as you type.
4. Use Reset to start over with the default values.

## Formula

Count full years, then full months, then remaining days between the two dates, borrowing from the previous month when needed.

That is the standard formula for this calculation, and it is the same one the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) applies to the values you enter. You can work it out by hand, but the calculator is faster when you want to try several values, and it removes the risk of a slip in the arithmetic. If the figures look wrong, check your inputs first — a misplaced decimal point is the usual culprit.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. Born 15 January 2000, on 10 March 2026 you are 26 years, 1 month and 23 days old. Try the same figures in the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves. The steps are the same whatever numbers you use; only the figures change.

## What the result shows

With the default values (Date of birth: 1995-06-15, Age on: today), the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) returns Age: 31 years 5 months 6 days, Total days: 11,482, Total weeks: 1,640, Next birthday in: 206 days. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. Change any value and every figure updates instantly. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Days Between Dates Calculator](/days-between-dates-calculator) is worth a look before you finish.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- Change the ''Age on'' date to see your age on any future date.
- Leap years are handled automatically.

Keep these in mind and the figures will be as useful as the numbers you put in.

## Frequently asked questions

### How do I calculate age in days?

Subtract the birth date from today; the calculator does this using real calendar dates. Try it with your own numbers in the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What if I was born on 29 February?

In non-leap years, legal birthdays are usually treated as 28 February or 1 March depending on the country. Try it with your own numbers in the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What formula does the Age Calculator use?

Count full years, then full months, then remaining days between the two dates, borrowing from the previous month when needed. You can see it in action in the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) — enter your own values and the result updates as you type.

### What do I need to use the Age Calculator?

Just Date of birth and Age on. Enter those values and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. If you are unsure about a value, try a rough figure first and then refine it — the result updates instantly.

### Is the Age Calculator free to use?

Yes. The Age Calculator is free to use as often as you like, with no sign-up. There is no limit on how often you use it, nothing to install, and no account to create — open the [Age Calculator](/age-calculator) and start straight away.

## Related tools

If this calculation is part of a bigger job, the other Date & Time tools on CalcHub can help. The [Days Between Dates Calculator](/days-between-dates-calculator), [Date Calculator](/date-calculator), [Time Calculator](/time-calculator) and [Week Number Calculator](/week-number-calculator) all work the same way — enter your values and the result appears instantly, free and with no sign-up. For more detail, read the guides to the [Days Between Dates Calculator](/blog/days-between-dates-calculator-guide) and [Date Calculator](/blog/date-calculator-guide), which explain how those calculations work and when to use them.

## Try it now

