1. Enter your radius.
2. Check the result — it updates instantly as you type.
3. Use Reset to start over with the default values.

## Formula

Area = πr²; circumference = 2πr; diameter = 2r.

That is the standard formula for this calculation, and it is the same one the [Circle Calculator](/circle-calculator) applies to the values you enter. You can work it out by hand, but the calculator is faster when you want to try several values, and it removes the risk of a slip in the arithmetic. If the figures look wrong, check your inputs first — a misplaced decimal point is the usual culprit.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. A radius of 5 gives area 78.5398 and circumference 31.4159. Try the same figures in the [Circle Calculator](/circle-calculator) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves. The steps are the same whatever numbers you use; only the figures change.

## What the result shows

With the default values (Radius: 5), the [Circle Calculator](/circle-calculator) returns Area: 78.53981634, Circumference: 31.41592654, Diameter: 10. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. Change any value and every figure updates instantly. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Percentage Calculator](/percentage-calculator) is worth a look before you finish.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- Check the units and labels before comparing results.
- Your entries stay in your browser and are not saved.

Keep these in mind and the figures will be as useful as the numbers you put in.

