INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category, tags, status, author, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('how-to-calculate-your-monthly-mortgage-payment', 'How to calculate your monthly mortgage payment', 'The mortgage formula, a worked example on a $350,000 loan, and what the payment leaves out — taxes, insurance and PMI.', 'A mortgage is usually the biggest monthly payment in a household budget, so it pays to understand exactly where the number comes from. The good news: the maths is a single formula, and once you know it you can check any lender''s figure in seconds.

## The formula

The monthly payment on a fixed-rate mortgage is:

```
M = P × r × (1 + r)ⁿ ÷ ((1 + r)ⁿ − 1)
```

- **P** is the amount you borrow (the principal)
- **r** is the monthly interest rate — the annual rate divided by 12, then by 100
- **n** is the total number of monthly payments (years × 12)

## A worked example

Take a $350,000 mortgage at 6.5% over 30 years:

- Monthly rate r = 6.5 ÷ 12 ÷ 100 = 0.005417
- Number of payments n = 30 × 12 = 360
- Monthly payment M ≈ **$2,212.24**

Over the full term you repay about $796,406 — roughly $446,406 of that is interest. That is why the rate and the term matter so much: a 1% lower rate on the same loan saves tens of thousands.

## What the payment does not include

The formula above covers **principal and interest only**. Your real monthly housing cost is usually higher, because most lenders also collect:

- **Property tax** — often 0.5–2% of the home''s value per year, depending on location
- **Homeowner''s insurance**
- **Private mortgage insurance (PMI)** if your down payment is under 20%
- **HOA fees**, where they apply

Add those to the principal-and-interest figure before deciding what you can afford.

## How to use the mortgage calculator

1. Enter the **loan amount** — the price minus your down payment.
2. Enter the **interest rate (yearly)** from your lender''s offer.
3. Enter the **term** in years (15, 20 or 30 are the most common).
4. Read the monthly payment, total interest and total repaid — then try a shorter term or a larger down payment and watch the interest fall.

## Three ways to pay less interest

- **Shorten the term.** A 15-year mortgage at the same rate has a higher payment but dramatically less total interest.
- **Pay a little extra each month.** Even $100 extra goes straight to the principal and shortens the loan.
- **Refinance when rates fall.** If rates drop by 0.75–1% and you plan to stay put, refinancing can be worth the closing costs.

Try it yourself with the [Mortgage Calculator](/mortgage-calculator), and compare it against the [Loan Calculator](/loan-calculator) and the [Compound Interest Calculator](/compound-interest-calculator) to see how borrowing and saving mirror each other.
', 'Finance', ARRAY['mortgage', 'home loan', 'interest', 'personal finance'], 'published', 'CalcHub', '2026-11-18T09:00:00.000Z', '2026-11-18T09:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, excerpt = EXCLUDED.excerpt, content = EXCLUDED.content, category = EXCLUDED.category, tags = EXCLUDED.tags, status = EXCLUDED.status, author = EXCLUDED.author, updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at;
INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category, tags, status, author, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('hreflang-tag-generator-guide', 'Hreflang Tag Generator: how it works, formula and examples', 'Hreflang Tag Generator: Generate hreflang tags for multilingual pages — one tag per language. Learn how to use it, the formula behind it, a worked example and…', 'The [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) is a free online tool on CalcHub. Generate hreflang tags for multilingual pages — one tag per language. Enter your URLs with language codes (one per line: URL, code) and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. Because it runs entirely in your browser, the values you type never leave your device, and you can use it as often as you like.

Generate hreflang tags for multilingual pages: enter each language version of a page with its language code, and copy the finished <link rel="alternate" hreflang="…"> tags into every version''s <head>. Hreflang tells search engines which language and region each version is for, so visitors land on the right one. Use codes like en, de, fr, or en-US for a region. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is uploaded. This guide explains how to use it, the formula it applies, and a worked example you can follow with your own numbers.

## How to use the Hreflang Tag Generator

Start by typing or pasting your URLs with language codes (one per line: URL, code). The result updates as you type, so adjust any value and watch the answer change. If the answer is not what you expected, check the value you entered first — a missing digit or a wrong unit is the usual cause. When you want a clean slate, Reset restores the defaults. The steps below walk through each input in order.

1. List each language version: its URL and its language code, one per line.
2. Add an x-default entry for the fallback page if you have one.
3. Copy the tags and paste them into the <head> of every language version.

## How it works

The [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) works straight from the values you enter: change an input and the result updates immediately, with no page reload and no waiting. Everything happens in your browser, so your entries never leave your device. Try it with a few different values and you will quickly get a feel for how the inputs move the result.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. Three versions (en, de, fr) produce three link tags — one per language — to paste into the <head> of each version, including a self-referencing tag for the page itself. Try the same figures in the [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves.

## Trying it out

The fastest way to see what the [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) does is to open it and enter values you already know the answer for. Change one input at a time and watch how the result responds — that tells you more than any description. Everything runs in your browser, so you can experiment freely: nothing is saved, sent or tracked.

## Language codes

Use ISO codes: en for English, de for German, fr for French, and optionally a region — en-US, en-GB, de-DE, fr-CA. x-default marks the fallback page shown when no language matches. Codes are case-sensitive in the usual form: language lowercase, region uppercase.

## The rule everyone forgets

Hreflang is reciprocal: every version of a page must link to every other version, including itself. If page A links to page B but B does not link back to A, search engines ignore the tags. Generate the full set once and paste the same set into every version.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- Every version must link to every version, including itself.
- Use x-default for the page visitors see when no language matches.
- Check the result with the hreflang testing tool in Search Console.

## Frequently asked questions

### What is hreflang?

An HTML tag that tells search engines a page has versions in different languages or for different regions, so the right version shows in each country''s results. Try it with your own numbers in the [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What language codes do I use?

ISO codes such as en, de, fr — with an optional region, en-US or en-GB. x-default marks the fallback page. Try it with your own numbers in the [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### Do the tags go on every version?

Yes. Each version of a page must carry the full set of tags, including a tag pointing at itself. One-way links are ignored. Try it with your own numbers in the [Hreflang Tag Generator](/hreflang-tag-generator) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.

### What do I need to use the Hreflang Tag Generator?

Just URLs with language codes (one per line: URL, code). Enter those values and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. If you are unsure about a value, try a rough figure first and then refine it — the result updates instantly.

## Related tools

The [Website SEO Audit](/website-seo-audit), [Broken Link Checker](/broken-link-checker), [Keyword Density Checker](/keyword-density-checker) and [Heading Checker](/heading-checker) cover related calculations in the same category. Read the guides to the [Website SEO Audit](/blog/website-seo-audit-guide) and [Broken Link Checker](/blog/broken-link-checker-guide) for more detail on how those work.

