1. Enter your hourly rate.
2. Enter your regular hours.
3. Enter your overtime hours.
4. Enter your overtime multiplier (×).
5. Check the result — it updates instantly as you type.
6. Use Reset to start over with the default values.

## Formula

Overtime pay = hourly rate × overtime hours × multiplier.

That is the standard formula for this calculation, and it is the same one the [Overtime Pay Calculator](/overtime-calculator) applies to the values you enter. You can work it out by hand, but the calculator is faster when you want to try several values, and it removes the risk of a slip in the arithmetic. If the figures look wrong, check your inputs first — a misplaced decimal point is the usual culprit.

## Worked example

Here is a typical example. 25/hour with 40 regular hours and 8 hours at 1.5× pays 1,300. Try the same figures in the [Overtime Pay Calculator](/overtime-calculator) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves. The steps are the same whatever numbers you use; only the figures change.

## What the result shows

With the default values (Hourly rate: 25, Regular hours: 40, Overtime hours: 8, Overtime multiplier: 1.5 ×), the [Overtime Pay Calculator](/overtime-calculator) returns Total weekly pay: 1,300, Regular pay: 1,000, Overtime pay: 300, Overtime hourly rate: 37.5. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. Change any value and every figure updates instantly. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Loan Calculator](/loan-calculator) is worth a look before you finish.

## Tips

A few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:

- Check the units and labels before comparing results.
- Your entries stay in your browser and are not saved.

Keep these in mind and the figures will be as useful as the numbers you put in.

## Frequently asked questions

